    "created_at": "2016-10-05T18:07:39Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-10-05T18:42:30Z",
    "body": "> I added a single .htaccess resource containing ForceType text/html in the commit-snapshots/ directory.\n\n@annevk That would be my solution if I could only use `.htaccess` files.\n\n## \n\nAs far as I remember, you  can not match directories from a `.htaccess` file. However, you can do it from [the main server configuration file (`httpd.conf`), or from inside a `<VirtualHost>` container](https://httpd.apache.org/docs/2.4/mod/directive-dict.html#Context).\n\ne.g. using the [`<LocationMatch>`](https://httpd.apache.org/docs/2.4/mod/core.html#locationmatch) directive:\n\n``` apache\n<LocationMatch \"^/commit-snapshots/*\">\n  ForceType text/html\n</LocationMatch>\n```\n\n## \n\nIf for some reason you really want to do it from the root `.htaccess` file, I guess you can just match the `SHA-1`:\n\n``` apache\n<FilesMatch \"^[0-9a-f]{40}$\">                                              \n  ForceType text/html                                                          \n</FilesMatch> \n```\n\n⚠️ Note: The above will try to match against all files (not only the ones from the `commit-snapshots` directory), so if you use it, you'll need to be careful not to have any other file types with names that match.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
